<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="1995 Colnago Super Thron: lugged Columbus steel with Campagnolo Chorus and Shamal wheels."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/colnago-super-thron/hero.webp"/><title>Colnago Super Thron 1995 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/colnago-super-thron/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">Previously in the collection · Cambiago, Italy · 1995</p><h1>Colnago<br/><em>Super Thron</em></h1><p>A traditional Italian steel frame whose integrated controls and deep wheels already pointed towards a new era.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Black 1995 Colnago Super Thron"/><figcaption>Campagnolo Chorus and deep Shamal rims. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>1995</strong><span>Stated year</span></div><div><strong>56 cm</strong><span>Frame size</span></div><div><strong>Thron Super</strong><span>Columbus steel</span></div><div><strong>Chorus</strong><span>Campagnolo groupset</span></div></section><section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">The Super name</p><h2>The old form continued</h2></div><div><p>The Columbus Thron Super decal identifies round tubes joined with lugs, unlike the Master’s profiled Gilco tubes. The supplied 1995 dating fits its finish and equipment, though Colnago model names from this period are not always consistent in surviving catalogues.</p><p>It was not Colnago’s most expensive steel frame. That makes it useful evidence of how the classic lugged racer continued while professional attention moved towards carbon.</p></div></section><section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">Campagnolo Chorus</p><h2>Shifting moved into the hands</h2></div><div><p>Ergopower combined braking and indexed shifting in one control. Polished aluminium visually connects the modern system with the frame’s chrome.</p><p>Deep aluminium Shamal rims made speed visible. They were heavy by present standards, but turned an aerodynamic experiment into a factory-built road wheel.</p></div></section><section class="atala-detail-pair"><figure><img src={`${images}/chorus-rear.webp`} alt="Campagnolo Chorus derailleur and Shamal wheel"/><figcaption>Chorus mechanics against the deep rim.</figcaption></figure><div><h2>Two periods on one bicycle</h2><p>The frame is quiet and traditional; the wheels are large, polished and boldly lettered. That tension gives the bicycle its character.</p></div></section><section class="atala-era"><p class="section-label">1995</p><blockquote>A Colnago C40 won Paris–Roubaix. <em>Steel continued anyway.</em></blockquote><div><p>Franco Ballerini’s victory showed carbon could win a brutal classic. Round steel tubes did not disappear overnight; they remained familiar, repairable and entirely capable of carrying modern components.</p></div></section><section class="koga-specs"><div><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Frame</dt><dd>Columbus Thron Super steel</dd></div><div><dt>Groupset</dt><dd>Campagnolo Chorus</dd></div><div><dt>Wheels</dt><dd>Campagnolo Shamal</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Previously in the collection</dd></div></dl></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>The photographs preserve a precise moment: classic steel, modern shifting and wheels that made no attempt to be discreet.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/1990-1999/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
